   $q$A parabola has x-intercepts at $x = 1$ and $x = 5$ and crosses the y-axis at $(0, 10)$. Which equation describes it?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$y = 10(x - 1)(x - 5)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-y-intercept-as-a"},{"text":"$y = 2(x + 1)(x + 5)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"flipped-signs-of-zeros"},{"text":"$y = (x - 1)(x - 5)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"forgot-to-solve-for-a"},{"text":"$y = 2(x - 1)(x - 5)$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$y = 2(x - 1)(x - 5)$$q$, $q$Zeros at $1$ and $5$ give $y = a(x - 1)(x - 5)$. At $x = 0$ this is $a(-1)(-5) = 5a$, and $5a = 10$ means $a = 2$. So $y = 2(x - 1)(x - 5)$. The choice $y = (x - 1)(x - 5)$ has the right zeros but crosses the y-axis at $(0, 5)$.$q$, 'draft', null)
